// A `for` inside a `for` reads a field the outer one sets of its own
// entities only as it was before the outer one started (`old`: a copy of
// the column is kept while it runs): each spot moves towards the middle
// of the others as they were. Without `old` the program is told; reading
// only fields that are not set needs none.
// RUN: %ent run %S/Inputs/for_in_for_old.ent 2>/dev/null > %t.out
// RUN: %ent run --parallel %S/Inputs/for_in_for_old.ent 2>/dev/null | cmp - %t.out
// RUN: FileCheck %s --input-file=%t.out
// RUN: sed 's/other: old Spot/other: Spot/' %S/Inputs/for_in_for_old.ent > %t.plain.ent
// RUN: not ent-translate --import-ent %t.plain.ent -I %S/../../devices -o /dev/null 2>&1 \
// RUN:   | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=PLAIN
// RUN: sed -e 's/other: old Spot/other: Spot/' -e 's/sum += other.x \* other.weight/sum += other.weight/' \
// RUN:     %S/Inputs/for_in_for_old.ent > %t.other.ent
// RUN: ent-translate --import-ent %t.other.ent -I %S/../../devices -o /dev/null

// PLAIN: error: this 'for' reads 'x' of 'Spot' of other entities, which the 'for' around sets of its own: some would be set already and some not. Bind it 'old' ('name: old Spot') to read what it was before that 'for' started (a copy is kept for it), or set another field and copy it over after

// CHECK:      1: 0.75
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: 1.625
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: 4.25
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: 8.625
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: 14.75
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: 1.40625
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: 2.171875
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: 4.46875
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: 8.296875
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: 13.65625
